1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,700
This is Doing and Feeling, the long version. Part 2, Feeling. Episode 7, The Hard Problem. In 1714, Gottfried Leibniz proposed a thought experiment that sits underneath every argument in this part of the series.

2
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:27,740
Suppose, he said, there were a machine whose structure made it think, feel, and perceive. Now imagine it enlarged, keeping all its proportions, until you could walk inside it the way you walk into a mill.

3
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:31,820
Inside, you would find only parts pushing against other parts.

4
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:35,120
Nowhere would you find anything that explains a perception.

5
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:40,800
Leibniz concluded that perception must belong to something simple, not to mechanism.

6
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:45,360
Swap the mill for a modern neural network, and you have the contemporary version.

7
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:52,200
Open up a large language model, and you find matrix multiplications and nonlinearities all the way down.

8
00:00:52,710 --> 00:00:54,600
You can trace every number it computes,

9
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:58,520
and nowhere in the trace is there a place where things start to feel like something.

10
00:00:59,060 --> 00:01:01,700
Open up a brain, and the story is the same,

11
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:05,380
with ion channels and neurotransmitters instead of matrices.

12
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:10,120
Notice the shape of the argument, because it's this whole episode in miniature.

13
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:16,340
It moves from I cannot see experience in the mechanism to experience is not in the mechanism.

14
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:20,160
The question for this episode is whether that move is valid

15
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:26,600
and what you're committed to if you say yes or no. Here's the route. A short history of how this

16
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:32,540
became a respectable problem. Then the three arguments that define the modern debate. Thomas

17
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:39,040
Nagel's bat, Joseph Levine's explanatory gap, and David Chalmers's hard problem, including the

18
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:45,000
formal conceivability argument and the full map of responses to it. Then Frank Jackson's Mary and

19
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,800
the four serious replies to her. Then the distinction between access and phenomenal

20
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:57,020
consciousness, and an experiment from 1960 that still divides the field. Then Wittgenstein. And

21
00:01:57,020 --> 00:02:01,400
finally, a first look at the meta-problem, which the next episode puts to work.

22
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:09,340
The problem has been noticed many times. In the 1860s, Thomas Huxley wrote that the arrival of a

23
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:15,280
state of consciousness from irritated nerve tissue was as unaccountable as the genie appearing when

24
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:23,000
Aladdin rubbed his lamp. In 1872, the physiologist Emel du Biwa-Raymond gave a famous lecture on the

25
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,980
limits of natural science, and consciousness was on his list. His verdict on how matter produces

26
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:35,700
sensation was a single Latin word, ignorabimus, we shall never know. Then psychology spent much

27
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:40,900
of the 20th century, avoiding the subject. Behaviorism said that science should study

28
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:47,840
behavior, not interstates. In 1949, the philosopher Gilbert Ryle mocked the picture of a ghost in the

29
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:53,460
machine as a category mistake, like a visitor who is shown the colleges, the libraries, and the

30
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:59,540
laboratories, and then asks where the university is. The cognitive revolution brought back internal

31
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:05,040
processing, but as information processing. Experience itself stayed off the agenda.

32
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:12,760
The modern revival came in the 1990s. In 1994, Francis Crick, co-discoverer of the structure

33
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:19,240
of DNA, published The Astonishing Hypothesis, arguing that your joys and sorrows, your memories,

34
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:24,800
your sense of self and free will, come down to the activity of enormous numbers of neurons.

35
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:34,120
That same year, at a conference in Tucson, a young Australian philosopher named David Chalmers gave a talk that named the problem in a way that stuck.

36
00:03:34,540 --> 00:03:39,740
Within a few years, there were dedicated journals, conferences, and a research program.

37
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:43,040
But the argument everyone reaches for first is older.

38
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:51,520
Thomas Nagel's 1974 essay, What Is It Like to Be a Bat?, is 16 pages long and makes four moves.

39
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:53,980
The fourth is the one people forget.

40
00:03:54,800 --> 00:04:00,320
First, a definition. A creature is conscious, Nagel says, when there is something it is like

41
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:05,940
to be that creature, for the creature itself. That phrase, something it is like, became the

42
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:11,120
standard way of picking out what philosophers call phenomenal consciousness. Second, that

43
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:16,420
subjective character is tied to a point of view. Bats perceive the world by echolocation,

44
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:22,840
they emit high-pitched calls and build a picture from the echoes. We can study sonar exhaustively,

45
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:30,720
that way is fully accessible only from a bat's kind of point of view. Imagining yourself hanging

46
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:36,060
upside down with webbed arms does not help. That tells you what it would be like for you to behave

47
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:42,520
like a bat, not what it is like for a bat to be a bat. Third, and this is the key step, scientific

48
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:47,860
reduction normally works by moving away from particular points of view toward more objective

49
00:04:47,860 --> 00:04:53,780
description. We understood lightning by setting aside how it looks to human eyes and describing

50
00:04:54,960 --> 00:05:00,580
charge. But if the thing you're trying to explain is the point of view itself, then moving away from

51
00:05:00,580 --> 00:05:06,060
points of view moves you away from the very thing you wanted to explain. Fourth, the conclusion.

52
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:12,160
Nagel did not say physicalism is false. He said that we currently have no idea how it could be

53
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:18,180
true. His analogy was the claim that matter is energy, put forward by a philosopher in ancient

54
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:24,420
Greece. It might have been true, but no one then had the concepts to see how. Nagel even proposed

55
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:30,760
program he called objective phenomenology, a way of describing the structure of experiences

56
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:36,220
that does not require having them. That program is still mostly a promissory note,

57
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:42,180
but it's worth remembering that the most famous anti-reductionist essay of the 20th century ends

58
00:05:42,180 --> 00:05:46,260
by asking for better science, not by declaring it impossible.

59
00:05:47,460 --> 00:05:51,780
Joseph Levine sharpened the point in 1983 with a phrase that stuck,

60
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:57,920
the explanatory gap. Compare two identity claims. Heat is the motion of molecules,

61
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:04,280
and pain is the firing of sea fibers, which, by the way, is a philosopher's toy example

62
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:09,260
rather than real neuroscience. In the heat case, once you understand molecular motion,

63
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:14,880
nothing is left over. Molecular motion does everything heat does. It raises thermometers,

64
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:20,940
melts ice, burns your hand. There's no residual question. In the pain case, even if the identity

65
00:06:21,780 --> 00:06:26,500
could still ask, why does that firing feel like this, rather than like something else or like

66
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:32,400
nothing at all? The identity might hold, but it does not explain. Levine's point was epistemic,

67
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:38,300
not metaphysical. He was not claiming that pain is non-physical. He was claiming that our

68
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:44,100
explanations leave a gap that our explanations of heat do not. Whether that gap reflects something

69
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:49,580
about the world or only about the limits of our concepts is what the rest of the debate is about.

70
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:53,820
Heat had a gap, too, until statistical mechanics closed it.

71
00:06:54,120 --> 00:06:56,220
Why think this one is different in kind?

72
00:06:56,640 --> 00:07:00,560
That's the hope of a position we'll meet shortly, Type-C physicalism.

73
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:03,040
The worry is about how the heat gap closed.

74
00:07:03,580 --> 00:07:07,340
Statistical mechanics showed that molecular motion does everything heat does.

75
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:11,700
It explained heat's functions, and for heat there was nothing more to explain.

76
00:07:12,040 --> 00:07:15,160
If experience is something over and above its functions,

77
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:18,940
a story about functions cannot close the gap the same way.

78
00:07:19,580 --> 00:07:23,020
Whether it is something more is exactly what Chalmers' argument is about.

79
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,800
Which brings us to Chalmers.

80
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:30,740
In his 1995 paper, Facing Up to the Problem of Consciousness,

81
00:07:30,900 --> 00:07:33,960
and his 1996 book, The Conscious Mind,

82
00:07:34,300 --> 00:07:38,020
he separated the easy problems of consciousness from the hard problem.

83
00:07:38,260 --> 00:07:40,940
The easy problems are about explaining functions,

84
00:07:41,320 --> 00:07:44,440
how we discriminate stimuli, integrate information,

85
00:07:44,860 --> 00:07:49,120
report our mental states, focus attention, deliberately control behavior,

86
00:07:49,580 --> 00:07:55,280
how waking differs from sleep. They're easy only in a special sense. They may take centuries to

87
00:07:55,280 --> 00:08:00,160
solve, but we know what kind of thing an answer is, a mechanism that performs the function.

88
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:05,420
The hard problem is why the performance of those functions is accompanied by experience.

89
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:11,320
Chalmers' claim is structural. Once you've explained every function, it still seems a

90
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:16,280
further question why any of it is felt. Function does not appear to entail feeling.

91
00:08:16,280 --> 00:08:19,600
That sounds like an argument from incredulity.

92
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:25,700
I cannot see how the code produces the behavior, so the behavior must come from somewhere else.

93
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:30,800
If a junior engineer said that about a bug, I'd send them back to the debugger.

94
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:33,800
And if that were the whole argument, you'd be right to.

95
00:08:34,020 --> 00:08:37,780
So Chalmers made it explicit, and it's worth going through slowly.

96
00:08:38,120 --> 00:08:40,360
This is the conceivability argument.

97
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:43,560
Premise 1. A zombie world is conceivable.

98
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:50,200
Picture a world physically identical to ours, down to the last particle, in which nobody experiences anything.

99
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:57,140
Your zombie twin talks about the redness of sunsets and the pain of stubbed toes, but there's nothing it's like to be it.

100
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:03,880
Not conceivable in a lazy sense, but ideally conceivable, surviving any amount of careful reflection.

101
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:09,400
Premise 2. If something is ideally conceivable, then it's metaphysically possible.

102
00:09:10,060 --> 00:09:10,700
Premise 3.

103
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:12,820
If a zombie world is possible,

104
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:16,700
then the physical facts do not necessitate the facts about experience,

105
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:18,480
and physicalism is false.

106
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:19,640
Conclusion.

107
00:09:19,980 --> 00:09:21,120
Physicalism is false.

108
00:09:21,500 --> 00:09:22,920
The argument is valid,

109
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:25,520
so every response has to reject a premise,

110
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:30,300
and Chalmers himself drew the map of responses in a 2003 paper

111
00:09:30,300 --> 00:09:32,320
that sorts positions by letter.

112
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:36,100
It's the most useful map in the field, so here it is.

113
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:38,700
Type of physicalists deny premise 1.

114
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:41,560
Daniel Dennett and the illusionists live here.

115
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:44,400
Zombies only seem conceivable, they say,

116
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:47,780
because we have not really imagined everything we're stipulating.

117
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:50,420
Dennett's comparison is vitalism.

118
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:54,460
Picture someone who, after every function of life has been explained,

119
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:58,000
still asks why all that is accompanied by life itself.

120
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:02,620
The question sounds deep until you realize that being alive just is metabolism,

121
00:10:03,160 --> 00:10:05,300
growth, reproduction, and the rest.

122
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:08,600
Once those are explained, nothing is left over.

123
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:14,900
this view, experience is the same. Explain every function and there's no further ingredient,

124
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:20,260
and zombies seem conceivable only because we never really imagine all the functions at once.

125
00:10:20,680 --> 00:10:27,080
Type B physicalists accept premise 1 but deny premise 2. Brian Lohr, David Papineau,

126
00:10:27,220 --> 00:10:33,200
and Ned Block are among them. Their model is water. Before modern chemistry, it seemed perfectly

127
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:38,880
conceivable that water was something other than H2O. But Saul Kripke showed that identity statements

128
00:10:39,400 --> 00:10:45,620
water is H2O, if true, are necessarily true, even though we discover them empirically.

129
00:10:45,840 --> 00:10:51,860
So conceivability can mislead. Maybe pain is identical to some brain process necessarily,

130
00:10:52,240 --> 00:10:56,880
even though we can conceive otherwise. That sounds like the obvious answer.

131
00:10:57,600 --> 00:11:03,120
Conceivability is a fact about our concepts, not about the world. So what stops type B

132
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:09,680
physicalism from simply winning. Partly Kripke himself, which is a lovely twist. In naming and

133
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:15,580
necessity, he argued that pain is not like water. With water, we can explain away the feeling of

134
00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:21,760
contingency. When we seem to imagine water that is not H2O, what we're really imagining is some

135
00:11:21,760 --> 00:11:28,760
watery-looking stuff that is not H2O fools water, so to speak. With pain, the trick fails. Anything

136
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:34,340
that feels like pain simply is pain. There's no gap between how pain appears and what it is,

137
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:40,300
so you cannot say you merely imagined something that seemed like pain. Kripke used this against

138
00:11:40,300 --> 00:11:45,920
identity theories, and type B physicalists have been working around it ever since. Chalmers added

139
00:11:45,920 --> 00:11:52,340
a formal tool, two-dimensional semantics, to say exactly when conceivability does entail possibility.

140
00:11:52,820 --> 00:11:58,260
Very roughly, it separates what a word picks out in the actual world from the way it picks things

141
00:11:59,120 --> 00:12:03,920
Type B physicalists reject the tool, and the dispute continues in technical journals.

142
00:12:04,540 --> 00:12:10,780
Type C physicalism says the gap is real now, but will close once we have concepts we do not yet possess.

143
00:12:11,580 --> 00:12:17,100
Chalmers argues that under pressure it collapses into one of the other letters, physicalist or not.

144
00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:19,620
Then come the non-physicalist letters.

145
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,440
Type D is interactionist dualism.

146
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:25,920
Experience is non-physical and pushes matter around.

147
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:28,400
Type E is epiphenomenalism.

148
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:31,900
Experience is non-physical and does nothing at all.

149
00:12:32,120 --> 00:12:37,600
And typef is Russellian monism, the family that includes many forms of panpsychism,

150
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:43,520
on which experience is the hidden intrinsic nature of what physics describes only from the outside.

151
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:45,920
Keep these six letters in mind.

152
00:12:46,620 --> 00:12:51,520
Typa gets the whole of the next episode, and the rest are the skeleton of episode 9.

153
00:12:52,660 --> 00:12:58,460
Frank Jackson's knowledge argument from 1982 approaches the same territory from another direction.

154
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:03,160
Mary is a brilliant scientist who knows every physical fact about color vision,

155
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:06,540
the wavelengths, the retinal cones, every neural pathway.

156
00:13:06,820 --> 00:13:09,620
But she has lived her whole life in a black and white room.

157
00:13:09,900 --> 00:13:12,380
One day she walks out and sees a ripe tomato.

158
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:14,020
Does she learn something new?

159
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:18,160
If she does, then knowing all the physical facts was not knowing all the facts.

160
00:13:18,520 --> 00:13:21,680
There are four main replies, and they're worth having straight.

161
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:25,860
The ability hypothesis from David Lewis and Lawrence Nemiro.

162
00:13:26,580 --> 00:13:28,760
Mary gains abilities rather than facts,

163
00:13:29,020 --> 00:13:32,640
the ability to imagine red, to recognize it, to remember it.

164
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:34,540
Know how, not know that.

165
00:13:34,780 --> 00:13:38,240
The objection is that it really does seem she learned something statable.

166
00:13:38,480 --> 00:13:40,200
That seeing red is like this.

167
00:13:40,560 --> 00:13:42,140
The acquaintance hypothesis.

168
00:13:42,600 --> 00:13:46,980
Mary becomes directly acquainted with a property she already knew all about,

169
00:13:47,240 --> 00:13:49,200
a new relation to an old property.

170
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:53,420
Then the most influential reply, the phenomenal concept strategy.

171
00:13:54,100 --> 00:13:59,460
Mary acquires a new concept, a recognitional concept that points at the experience from the inside,

172
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:01,380
something like this kind of state.

173
00:14:01,640 --> 00:14:06,900
That new concept picks out a physical property she already knew under a different, scientific concept,

174
00:14:07,140 --> 00:14:11,400
the way the morning star and the evening star turned out to be the same planet.

175
00:14:11,820 --> 00:14:14,260
Same fact, new way of thinking about it.

176
00:14:14,500 --> 00:14:16,560
The strategy has a second attraction.

177
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:22,020
If phenomenal concepts and physical concepts are wired up independently in our heads,

178
00:14:23,420 --> 00:14:28,480
able to pull them apart in imagination, even if the things they refer to cannot come apart.

179
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:33,940
So the strategy explains why zombies seem conceivable without adding anything to the

180
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:39,760
world. It explains the intuition rather than honoring it. That's the move that feels right

181
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:46,440
to me as an engineer. When a model is confidently wrong, you explain the confidence. You do not

182
00:14:46,440 --> 00:14:52,920
treat it as evidence. It's attractive for exactly that reason. Chalmers replied in 2007,

183
00:14:53,420 --> 00:14:56,780
with what's sometimes called his master argument, which is a dilemma.

184
00:14:57,180 --> 00:15:01,520
Ask whether your zombie twin has the same phenomenal concepts you have.

185
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:06,760
If it does, then those concepts cannot explain your special epistemic situation,

186
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:09,780
since the zombie has them without any experience.

187
00:15:10,020 --> 00:15:15,280
If it does not, then phenomenal concepts cannot be explained in physical terms after all.

188
00:15:15,720 --> 00:15:18,460
Either way, he argues, the strategy fails.

189
00:15:18,960 --> 00:15:23,920
Its defenders have replies, and this is one of the liveliest disputes in the field.

190
00:15:24,240 --> 00:15:26,060
The fourth reply is Dennett's.

191
00:15:26,280 --> 00:15:30,640
He argued that a Mary who truly knew everything physical would not be surprised at all.

192
00:15:30,840 --> 00:15:34,280
Show her a blue banana as a trick, and she'd say at once,

193
00:15:34,480 --> 00:15:36,420
that is not what yellow looks like.

194
00:15:36,620 --> 00:15:37,740
And one more twist.

195
00:15:38,040 --> 00:15:42,180
Jackson himself later abandoned the argument and became a physicalist,

196
00:15:42,340 --> 00:15:45,520
explaining experience in terms of mental representation.

197
00:15:45,520 --> 00:15:48,760
The argument outlived its author's belief in it.

198
00:15:49,840 --> 00:15:54,000
Next, a distinction that quietly causes half the confusion in this area.

199
00:15:54,340 --> 00:16:01,420
In 1995, Ned Block distinguished phenomenal consciousness, the what it's like, from access consciousness.

200
00:16:01,860 --> 00:16:06,920
Information being available for reasoning, for report, and for controlling behavior.

201
00:16:07,420 --> 00:16:13,580
Block's point was that the two are conceptually distinct, and that scientists constantly slide between them.

202
00:16:15,560 --> 00:16:21,720
which we will meet properly in episode 10. People with damage to visual cortex who deny seeing

203
00:16:21,720 --> 00:16:26,920
anything in part of their visual field yet can guess about objects they're far better than chance.

204
00:16:27,260 --> 00:16:32,540
It's tempting to reason that because these patients lack visual experience and also lack

205
00:16:32,540 --> 00:16:38,580
certain abilities, experience must be what supplies those abilities. Block called this a fallacy.

206
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:44,460
The patients also lack access. You cannot tell which missing ingredient explains the missing

207
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:50,120
Bloch then went further and argued that phenomenal consciousness overflows access.

208
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:54,900
His evidence starts with an experiment by George Sperling from 1960.

209
00:16:55,460 --> 00:17:00,460
Flash a grid of 12 letters, three rows of four, for a fraction of a second.

210
00:17:00,660 --> 00:17:04,120
Ask people to report everything, and they manage about four.

211
00:17:04,360 --> 00:17:08,180
But if a tone just after the flash tells them which row to report,

212
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:11,640
they can get most of that row, whichever row it is.

213
00:17:11,820 --> 00:17:14,580
So for a moment, most of the grid was available,

214
00:17:15,520 --> 00:17:20,720
only a fraction can ever be reported. Block's interpretation is that you consciously see the

215
00:17:20,720 --> 00:17:26,900
whole grid, specific letters, and all, and that what's limited is access. Experience is richer

216
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:33,240
than report. He made the case in 2007 in a Target article in Behavioral and Brain Sciences,

217
00:17:33,580 --> 00:17:38,920
the format we met in episode 5, and its commentaries are a fine place to watch a field

218
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:44,720
argue with itself. On the other side, Michael Cohen and Daniel Dennett argued in 2011 that

219
00:17:45,820 --> 00:17:51,600
inaccessible in principle could never be tested. Others, such as the philosopher Ian Phillips,

220
00:17:52,140 --> 00:17:57,180
suggested that people may experience something more generic, letter-like shapes rather than

221
00:17:57,180 --> 00:18:02,560
specific letters. Underneath, this lies what's often called the refrigerator light problem.

222
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:07,960
Whenever you check whether you're conscious of something, you access it. So you can never catch

223
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:13,620
experience in the act of existing without access, just as you can never open the fridge door and

224
00:18:15,520 --> 00:18:18,480
This is a measurement problem wearing a metaphysics costume.

225
00:18:18,840 --> 00:18:21,760
Every probe you can build is an access probe.

226
00:18:22,280 --> 00:18:23,460
Largely, yes.

227
00:18:23,660 --> 00:18:27,140
And it becomes a major empirical fight in episode 11

228
00:18:27,140 --> 00:18:31,600
because two leading scientific theories line up on opposite sides of it.

229
00:18:31,800 --> 00:18:35,040
A few more distinctions will keep the rest of the series clear.

230
00:18:35,860 --> 00:18:37,800
Philosopher's separate creature consciousness,

231
00:18:38,120 --> 00:18:41,380
being awake rather than in a coma, from state consciousness,

232
00:18:41,660 --> 00:18:45,200
a particular mental state being conscious rather than unconscious.

233
00:18:46,040 --> 00:18:51,000
Scientists separate the level of consciousness, its contents, and the conscious self.

234
00:18:51,160 --> 00:18:56,580
And some researchers, including Tim Bain, argue that even talking about levels is misleading.

235
00:18:57,600 --> 00:19:02,780
Dreaming, psychedelic states, sedation, and minimal consciousness after brain injury

236
00:19:02,780 --> 00:19:07,960
differ along several dimensions at once, rather than sitting at different points on one dial.

237
00:19:09,220 --> 00:19:12,080
Ludwig Wittgenstein enters the story from an angle.

238
00:19:12,560 --> 00:19:14,240
In the philosophical investigations,

239
00:19:14,680 --> 00:19:18,540
he imagines that everyone has a box containing something they call a beetle.

240
00:19:18,980 --> 00:19:21,440
No one can look into anyone else's box,

241
00:19:21,460 --> 00:19:24,600
and everyone knows what a beetle is only from their own case.

242
00:19:24,880 --> 00:19:27,360
His point is that in such a language game,

243
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:30,300
the thing in the box drops out of the picture entirely.

244
00:19:30,800 --> 00:19:33,580
The word beetle gets its meaning from its public use,

245
00:19:33,800 --> 00:19:35,700
whatever is or is not inside.

246
00:19:36,140 --> 00:19:39,640
That image belongs to his larger private language argument.

247
00:19:39,640 --> 00:19:45,180
the claim that words for sensations cannot get their meaning from private inner objects

248
00:19:45,180 --> 00:19:47,380
that only the speaker can inspect.

249
00:19:47,680 --> 00:19:51,100
And in a passage that every student of this problem should read,

250
00:19:51,340 --> 00:19:56,380
he describes the feeling of an unbridgeable gulf between consciousness and brain processes.

251
00:19:56,720 --> 00:19:59,520
It does not arise in ordinary life, he says.

252
00:19:59,740 --> 00:20:03,060
It arises when you turn your attention on your own consciousness,

253
00:20:03,380 --> 00:20:06,280
in a philosophical mood, and ask in astonishment

254
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:09,260
how this could possibly come from a process in the brain.

255
00:20:09,640 --> 00:20:13,200
Notice the word this and the gesture inward that goes with it.

256
00:20:13,900 --> 00:20:19,400
Wittgenstein treated that astonishment as a symptom to be examined, not a discovery to be honored.

257
00:20:20,280 --> 00:20:27,480
Wittgenstein's influence runs through behaviorism, through Dennett, and into the contemporary view called illusionism.

258
00:20:27,780 --> 00:20:30,080
He did not try to solve the hard problem.

259
00:20:30,260 --> 00:20:33,040
He tried to show that it might be a knot in our language.

260
00:20:34,180 --> 00:20:39,520
That astonishment, the inward gesture, and the sense of a gulf is itself something people do.

261
00:20:39,640 --> 00:20:43,240
They say it, write it down, and argue about it on podcasts.

262
00:20:43,560 --> 00:20:48,020
In 2018, Chalmers turned that observation into a research program.

263
00:20:48,620 --> 00:20:51,680
Alongside the hard problem, he proposed the meta-problem.

264
00:20:52,100 --> 00:20:54,620
Explain why we think there is a hard problem.

265
00:20:54,760 --> 00:20:59,000
Why do brains produce reports like I cannot explain my own experience?

266
00:20:59,300 --> 00:21:01,280
Because those reports are behavior.

267
00:21:01,660 --> 00:21:05,040
Explaining them is, in principle, an easy problem,

268
00:21:05,040 --> 00:21:10,100
one that realists and their opponents can work on together while expecting different kinds of

269
00:21:10,100 --> 00:21:14,460
answer. I think it's the most productive development in this debate in decades,

270
00:21:14,460 --> 00:21:20,820
and the next episode builds on it. Let me step back and name the strategies on the table.

271
00:21:21,060 --> 00:21:26,320
The first is to dissolve the problem. Show that it rests on a confusion or an illusion.

272
00:21:26,840 --> 00:21:31,760
That's Wittgenstein's spirit and the illusionist program of Dennett and Keith Frankish.

273
00:21:31,760 --> 00:21:38,940
The second is to reduce the problem. Accept that experience is real and physical and explain why it

274
00:21:38,940 --> 00:21:44,640
seems otherwise. That's type B physicalism, the phenomenal concept strategy, and the

275
00:21:44,640 --> 00:21:49,440
representationalist theories that identify experience with a certain kind of mental

276
00:21:49,440 --> 00:21:55,680
representation. The third is to expand our picture of reality. Accept that physics as we know it

277
00:21:55,680 --> 00:22:02,320
leave something out and add to it. Dualism adds a new kind of property. Panpsychism and Russellian

278
00:22:02,320 --> 00:22:09,280
monism say experience is the intrinsic nature of matter. Idealism says experience is all there is.

279
00:22:09,780 --> 00:22:14,720
And where do professional philosophers actually land? Is there a majority view?

280
00:22:15,040 --> 00:22:20,720
There's survey data. In the 2020 Philpapers Survey of Professional Philosophers,

281
00:22:20,720 --> 00:22:27,180
about 62% said there is a hard problem of consciousness, and about 30% said there is not.

282
00:22:27,460 --> 00:22:33,560
At the same time, about 52% accepted or leaned toward physicalism about the mind,

283
00:22:33,780 --> 00:22:40,360
against 32% for non-physicalism. On consciousness specifically, functionalism was the most popular

284
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:45,560
single view at about a third, then dualism at about a fifth, with identity theory,

285
00:22:45,560 --> 00:22:48,300
panpsychism, and eliminativism trailing.

286
00:22:48,740 --> 00:22:51,640
Put that together and you get the field's real position.

287
00:22:52,040 --> 00:22:54,660
Most philosophers think the problem is genuine

288
00:22:54,660 --> 00:22:58,160
and a narrow majority still expect a physical answer.

289
00:22:58,620 --> 00:23:00,560
Few think it has been delivered yet.

290
00:23:01,480 --> 00:23:04,980
Eliminativism is the one that says consciousness does not exist.

291
00:23:05,280 --> 00:23:09,100
With under 5%, why give it more airtime than the others?

292
00:23:09,460 --> 00:23:11,780
Because the small number hides two things.

293
00:23:12,340 --> 00:23:16,580
First, the label eliminativism sounds like denying that you feel pain,

294
00:23:16,780 --> 00:23:19,180
which no serious defender of the view does.

295
00:23:19,580 --> 00:23:22,960
What they deny is that experience has the special private,

296
00:23:23,280 --> 00:23:25,900
ineffable qualities philosophers call qualia.

297
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:30,160
Critics reply that this is denying consciousness by another name.

298
00:23:30,520 --> 00:23:33,180
Most defenders prefer the label illusionism.

299
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:37,060
Second, it's the position that most changes the question we care about.

300
00:23:37,340 --> 00:23:39,020
If illusionism is right,

301
00:23:39,020 --> 00:23:42,220
asking whether a machine is conscious stops being a mystery

302
00:23:42,220 --> 00:23:46,920
and becomes a question you could, in principle, answer by looking inside it.

303
00:23:47,100 --> 00:23:51,960
So next episode, we give the most radical answer the strongest hearing we can

304
00:23:51,960 --> 00:23:54,480
and then put it through its hardest objections.

